A||||17||RFMike||This is even too loud for a deaf guy||||1||
Z||000000||RFMike||04-03-2000||09:52 PM||||What have you guys installed for exhaust systems?  My muffler (possibly OE, with 153K), has gotten REALLY loud. Also a small hole behind the cat.   My wife doesn't want to ride with me, the wimp.<BR>I have checked out some prices, and it ain't cheap.  I would settle for stock parts, but need a better source for them.  Lotsa cash, wherever I have looked.  Stainless steel is tempting, but $$$$$.  Besides, if I get anywhere near the miles out of new pipes that this set got, I'll be happy.  Somebody must have a good line on exhaust parts.<BR>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000001||Patrick's Dad||04-03-2000||10:47 PM||||I got mine from IRM. An oversized cat, then pipes, then four Hooker resonator tips. About $400 for the whole setup, plus install.<P>It's not quiet. The shop asked me if I wanted to reinstall the muffler. I said no. Aside from having to turn up the stereo a bit more, I think that it's a blast. A bit boomy at 2500RPM (Probably resonance of some sort), but it's sweet from 3500 on up. And the Police in Liberal Central (Metro Boston) don't seem to give me a second listen.<P>The Hookers do rust pretty quickly, though, and I've lost two of the four due to bad welding. Interestingly, the sound didn't change that much....<P>Will I replace it with something quieter now that it's coming off the road in a couple months for a makeover, and I'm heading to this side of middle age? I'm not convinced. In fact, I'm buying a '73 Plymouth Valiant w/a 318, and I'm thinking dual exhaust with a crossover pipe, and, maybe, no mufflers....||||reg||12
Z||000002||Bruce||04-03-2000||11:15 PM||||Here are the results from some of my searching on the same topic:<BR>Catco High Flow cat. converter: $90 R&E Racing<BR>Walker muffler #40094-includes most hardware):$109 Wrenchhead.com<BR>Borla Turbo Muffler #40651: $105,Carparts.com<BR>Dynomax muffler (#17777) $57, R&E Racing<BR>Flomaster Delta Flow Series 40: $140, West Coast Fieros<BR>I have only installed the cat so far, but it's very well made, and I notice a more powerful sound with better perfomance.<P>All subjective stuff, this exhaust business, so good luck!!<BR>bb||||reg||1
Z||000003||RFMike||04-04-2000||09:21 AM||||I will be installing the whole system from the cat to the tips.   The Fiero Store lists the Gorlic system for $180.  I read through all the old posts, and I know some of you have installed this system.<P>How do the tips look?  Are they oem style?<P>How do they sound?  My days of liking a loud car left me with my 71 Camaro.  21 years ago.<P>If you ordered the Fiero Store system, how long did it take?  My ride is now loud enough to get me pulled over.<P>Does anybody know of anyplace with a better price, or better system?  From previous posts, the Ocelot system was loud and expensive.<P>Thanks for any input,<BR>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000004||mwbackus||04-04-2000||10:30 AM||||Dont know of a cheaper cat. back system, but there are MUCH better prices on most exhaust components at Summit ( <A HREF="http://www.summitracing.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.summitracing.com/</A>  ).<P>For example: <BR>The Catco Cat. converter #CTO-6004 is   $50.00, and the Flowmaster Delta Flow Series 40 #FLO-9424412 is only 72.95!  <P>The stock exhaust plumbing is stainless steel, so it would be to your advantage to retain as much of it as possible (ie.. just install a new cat., muffler, and tips).<P>As for the tips, there are obviously many options, but I find that the $35 Fiero stores tips to be one of the best values.  I believe they are the same tips used on the Ocelot system, and you can see them for yourself here:   <A HREF="http://www.fierostore.com/nl/pg4/pg4.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fierostore.com/nl/pg4/pg4.html</A> <P><BR>Mike<P><p>[This message has been edited by mwbackus (edited 04-04-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||WKDFIRO||04-04-2000||12:47 PM||||Don't forget to just ask your local muffler shop how much it will cost for a 2.5 inch exhaust fabrication. It is possible for the cost to be really low depending on the place you go!||||reg||1
Z||000006||loafer87gt||04-04-2000||01:00 PM||||Is that Borla Turbo muffler from carparts the same one that is being included for with the Borla exhaust package from Fiero Designs and Appraisals? ||||reg||1
Z||000007||Bruce||04-04-2000||07:51 PM||||I'm not certain, but I would think the Borla muffler listed by Carparts would have to be the same as the one included in George's system, simply because so few things are made specifically for the Fiero.<BR>Speaking of which: Mike, is the Delta Series muffler you listed designed for the Fiero, or are there modifications that have to be made?<BR>Thanks,<BR>bb||||reg||1
Z||000008||88formula||04-04-2000||09:27 PM||||You guys keep tuned because I have info on the Borla exhaust coming in a couple of days with pictures. It has the chrome tips.||||reg||1
Z||000009||DXR_DAD||04-04-2000||10:31 PM||||Bruce is the flowmaster that mwbackus mentioned the same as the one you have for sale? Different part# ?  are they both 40's?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||Bruce||04-04-2000||11:23 PM||||If you look up the West Coast Fiero website, they have a Flowmaster muffler that is made for the Fiero. It's fairly expensive, though:$140. Mine is the one designed for the 82-92 Firebird/Camaro and it takes a little bit of modification to make it fit. It's the 80 series, which supposedly puts out a pretty good rumble. <BR>If we can find out from Mike (Backus) about the Delta Series he talks about for $72, maybe we're one step closer.<BR>bb||||reg||1
Z||000011||RFMike||04-04-2000||11:50 PM||||A few thoughts here:<P>I have read that the Fiero pipes are stainless, but a low grade.  My pipes look like they need replacing, and the hole that I do have reinforces that thought.<P>When you get pricing on a "muffler" for this car, many of the manufacturers include the inlet and exit pipes with it.  These tend to run $120-$180, depending on quality.  It is tough to know what the price icludes, unless you talk with somebody directly.  Even then, many over the counter people don't know.  The NAPA guys knew, the Checker guys didn't.  If I could get the muffler and related welded pipes for $75, I wouldn't be typing this now.  If anybody runs across that kind of pricing, I am definitely interested.  However, I have looked at Summit, Carparts.com, Wrenchhead, etc., and that isn't the case.  I am looking for a bolt on system, and if you only get a muffler, you have some serious welding to do, as far as I can tell.<BR>Please tell me I'm wrong on this one.  Normally I hate that, but here it would feel good.<P>The Fiero Store, from what I can tell, is more expensive on just about everthing.  In this case, they have the best price I've seen so far for what I'm looking for.<BR>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000012||Bruce||04-05-2000||12:43 AM||||The Walker muffler(40094)does include the pipes, according to Wrenchhead. I don't think any of the others do. I'm just curious about their quality and sound. Anyone out there with some Walker experience?||||reg||1
Z||000013||mwbackus||04-05-2000||02:26 AM||||I believe the Flowmaster Delta Flo I referred to in my post above is the same one offered by West Coast.  The Borla #40651 is a single in/out muffler and will not work for stock exhaust design installations.  The Walker is a "stock" muffler (in fact it is exactly the same as the stock unit it came with).  The aftermarket high flow units that are available are usually intended for the Camaro/Firebird and they are slightly longer than the original Fiero unit (it would be a far worse situation if it were shorter  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> )  It is really no big deal for a good muffler shop to install the longer units, and it should not cost any more than a stock replacement. Inlets are usually available in diameters ranging from stock up to 3".<P>Mike B.||||reg||1
Z||000014||RFMike||04-05-2000||07:50 AM||||Mike,<BR>I realize it's not a big deal for a shop to install a longer muffler.  Most shops want about $400 to do the work I want done.  I don't like doing exhaust work, but for $220 I'll crawl under there.  That's why I'm looking for a bolt up.<BR>Any suggestions?||||reg||1
Z||000015||mwbackus||04-05-2000||08:49 AM||||If you wanted to obtain something at least equal to the stock system, you would need to go with stainless.  Perhaps that Borla system that was the big deal a while ago is still on the table?  There is also a real expensive ($680) setup from RS Motorsports ( <A HREF="http://webhome.idirect.com/~rsm1/" TARGET=_blank>http://webhome.idirect.com/~rsm1/</A>  ) that would probably be a bolt in.<BR>$400 is way too much to replicate the system in aluminized unless they are doing all mandrel bends.  You may want to check with a few different shops.<P>Mike B.||||reg||1
Z||000016||Gokart||04-05-2000||08:18 PM||||RFMike.....I got the Fiero Store's Gorlic exhaust system and I'm happy with it but I don't think the tips are oem. They look alright but they are not as wide.||||reg||1
Z||000017||Dan86gt3.8||04-06-2000||02:32 AM||||well when I had the 2.8n I bought the ocelot,, it was worthless in my opinion.  It almost sounded like a ricer fart can,  But things have changed with time and now I am running a single cat into 2 dual resonarator tips.  Needles to say I make quite the racket.  My new favorite past time is setting off car alarms, I raise all kinds of hell in the parking garages around school, and most of the time I m not even trying  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">!!!||||reg||1
